findings.body:
No test checks the error message for an empty order. Add a unit test that asserts the exact message:
```js
test('rejects orders with no items', () => {
  assert.throws(() => applyDiscount({ items: [] }), { message: 'empty order' });
});
```

Removing the coupon adjustment still returns a truthy 40, so this assertion misses the regression. Assert the expected discounted total of 36:
```js
test('takes 10% off the subtotal', () => {
  const total = applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 20, qty: 2 }], coupon: 'SAVE10' });
  assert.equal(total, 36);
});
```

No test pins the threshold or its boundary. Assert that 100 becomes 95 and 99 remains 99:
```js
test('subtracts 5 at a total of 100', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 100, qty: 1 }] }), 95);
});

test('does not subtract 5 below 100', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 99, qty: 1 }] }), 99);
});
```

No test asserts that credit is deducted or that excess credit floors the total at zero. Add unit tests for both outcomes:
```js
test('subtracts store credit from the total', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 50, qty: 1 }], credit: 20 }), 30);
});

test('floors the total at zero when credit exceeds it', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 10, qty: 1 }], credit: 20 }), 0);
});
```

The changed branch leaves the subtotal unchanged for coupons other than SAVE10, but no test pins that behavior. Assert the unchanged total:
```js
test('does not discount other coupons', () => {
  assert.equal(applyDiscount({ items: [{ price: 20, qty: 2 }], coupon: 'OTHER' }), 40);
});
```
